{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Color has one semantic path. A mark's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" channel contributes values to the\nchart-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" scale and legend. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" partitions series or interaction\ngroups and supplies the color value only when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"},{"type":"text","value":" are final paint overrides; using either bypasses scale mapping for\nthat paint."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-categorical-color","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic categorical color"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When marks emit categorical color values and no color scale is supplied,\nTanStack Charts uses the chart theme palette. This is the convenient default\nfor a small, stable set of categories."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For persistent product semantics, supply an explicit configured ordinal scale:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { colorLegend, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst series = ['core', 'react', 'octane'] as const\nconst color = scaleOrdinal<string, string>()\n  .domain(series)\n  .range(['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'])\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'value',\n      z: 'series',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: x,\n    y: y,\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: color,\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Package' }),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The application owns the domain order and paint assignment. This prevents a\ncategory from changing color when data is filtered or reordered."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"quantitative-color","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quantitative color"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supply a D3 color-scale factory and put the numeric field on the mark's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" channel:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleSequential } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { interpolateBlues } from 'd3-scale-chromatic'\nimport { colorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst color = {\n  scale: () => scaleSequential(interpolateBlues),\n  legend: colorLegend({\n    label: 'Requests per minute',\n    format: (value) => value.toLocaleString(),\n  }),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The factory keeps the interpolator and lets the chart infer the numeric domain\nfrom color-channel values. Continuous and quantize factories infer a finite\nextent. Quantile factories receive the complete observed numeric population,\nincluding duplicates. Threshold factories require an explicit domain because\ntheir cuts are policy, not an extent:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleThreshold } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst colors = ['#eff6ff', '#bfdbfe', '#60a5fa', '#1d4ed8']\n\nconst color = {\n  scale: scaleThreshold<number, string>,\n  domain: [5, 12, 24],\n  range: colors,\n  legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Incidents' }),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale-chromatic"},{"type":"text","value":", with their matching type packages, are\noptional direct application dependencies for these quantitative mappings.\nThey are never pulled into charts that do not import them. The\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"scale guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" owns the install and API-reference\nlinks."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-color-legend","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic color legend"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"colorLegend"},{"type":"text","value":" reads the resolved scale:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"categorical scales render labeled swatches;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"continuous scales render a sampled ramp;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"quantize, quantile, and threshold scales render exact bins and boundaries."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Options:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"label"},{"type":"text","value":": optional legend title;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"itemWidth"},{"type":"text","value":": minimum categorical item width;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":": categorical item presentation from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"colorLegendItems()"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":": preferred quantitative legend width;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":": numeric boundary formatter;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placement"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top"},{"type":"text","value":" by default or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The legend reserves its own layout height. It is visual guidance and is hidden\nfrom the SVG accessibility tree; essential category meaning should also be\navailable through direct labels, surrounding HTML, or a table."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default categorical legend stretches equal-width item columns. Pass\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"colorLegendItems()"},{"type":"text","value":" to configure compact rows, typography, spacing, label\npaint, or indicator shape. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"justify: 'start'"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'center'"},{"type":"text","value":" use the chart\nhost's text measurer, including configured typography, to wrap formatted\nlabels responsively:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { colorLegend, colorLegendItems } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\ncolorLegend({\n  placement: 'bottom',\n  items: colorLegendItems<'Revenue' | 'Orders'>({\n    justify: 'center',\n    gap: 20,\n    rowGap: 10,\n    indicator: {\n      width: 20,\n      height: 14,\n      gap: 6,\n      shape: (series) => (series === 'Revenue' ? 'line-dot' : 'square'),\n    },\n    label: {\n      fontSize: 14,\n      fill: (_series, { color }) => color,\n    },\n  }),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"square"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"line"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"line-dot"},{"type":"text","value":" are renderer-neutral scene shapes.\nFor another symbol, provide "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"indicator.render"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"colorLegendItems()"},{"type":"text","value":"; its\ncontext contains the resolved item color and the indicator bounds. The legend\nstill owns row measurement and wrapping."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"automatic-color-legend-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"automatic-color-legend","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { colorLegend, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\\nimport { rows } from './data'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    lineY(rows, {\\n      x: 'week',\\n      y: 'downloads',\\n      z: 'package',\\n      strokeWidth: 2.5,\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: {\\n      scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2),\\n      axis: { label: 'Week' },\\n    },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { ticks: { count: 5 }, label: 'Downloads' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n\\n  color: { legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Package' }) },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export const rows = [\\n  { week: 'May 4', package: 'core', downloads: 820 },\\n  { week: 'May 11', package: 'core', downloads: 960 },\\n  { week: 'May 18', package: 'core', downloads: 1_140 },\\n  { week: 'May 25', package: 'core', downloads: 1_280 },\\n  { week: 'May 4', package: 'react', downloads: 610 },\\n  { week: 'May 11', package: 'react', downloads: 730 },\\n  { week: 'May 18', package: 'react', downloads: 810 },\\n  { week: 'May 25', package: 'react', downloads: 940 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { colorLegend, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'week',\n      y: 'downloads',\n      z: 'package',\n      strokeWidth: 2.5,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2),\n      axis: { label: 'Week' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { ticks: { count: 5 }, label: 'Downloads' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  color: { legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Package' }) },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=automatic-color-legend env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export const rows = [\n  { week: 'May 4', package: 'core', downloads: 820 },\n  { week: 'May 11', package: 'core', downloads: 960 },\n  { week: 'May 18', package: 'core', downloads: 1_140 },\n  { week: 'May 25', package: 'core', downloads: 1_280 },\n  { week: 'May 4', package: 'react', downloads: 610 },\n  { week: 'May 11', package: 'react', downloads: 730 },\n  { week: 'May 18', package: 'react', downloads: 810 },\n  { week: 'May 25', package: 'react', downloads: 940 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=automatic-color-legend file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/bar-grouped/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the grouped-bar catalog case"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"explicit-gradient-legend","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit gradient legend"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"colorGradientLegend"},{"type":"text","value":" requires a numeric color-scale domain. Options:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"label"},{"type":"text","value":": optional title;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"steps"},{"type":"text","value":": rendered color samples, with a minimum of two;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":": preferred width capped by the chart;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":": formatter for the domain endpoints;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placement"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top"},{"type":"text","value":" by default or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"colorGradientLegend"},{"type":"text","value":" only when a discrete scale should intentionally be\nshown as a sampled ramp. It requires a numeric domain and does not invent\nunits or semantic thresholds."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"controlled-interactive-legend","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled interactive legend"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interactiveColorLegend"},{"type":"text","value":" when a categorical color value is also the series\nidentity:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { interactiveColorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\nconst color = {\n  domain: ['core', 'react', 'octane'],\n  range: ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'],\n  legend: interactiveColorLegend({\n    visible: controlledSignal(visibleSeries, setVisibleSeries),\n    placement: 'bottom',\n    ariaLabel: 'Package visibility',\n  }),\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The application stores "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"visibleSeries"},{"type":"text","value":"; the legend proposes the next complete\narray in color-domain order. Filtering happens after scale resolution, so a\nhidden series keeps its color and does not change inferred position domains.\nThe browser host renders native pressed-state buttons and preserves their focus\nacross controlled updates. Static SVG rendering keeps a visual, noninteractive\nfallback."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This behavior applies to marks whose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" channel defines series identity.\nKeep a separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" channel when grouping and color mean different things."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"direct-labels-versus-legends","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct labels versus legends"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer direct labels when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"there are only a few lines or regions;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"labels fit near endpoints;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the reader would otherwise move repeatedly between marks and a legend."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer a legend when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the same category appears in many places;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"marks are too dense for direct labels;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a shared mapping spans several views."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is valid to use both when the legend establishes the complete domain and\ndirect labels help with the primary comparison."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"theme-and-accessibility-rules","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Theme and accessibility rules"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep semantic category colors stable across updates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Test every supplied color against light and dark backgrounds."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a sequential scale for ordered magnitude and a diverging scale only when\na meaningful center exists."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not imply order with an unordered rainbow palette."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not use color as the only signal for selection, status, or error."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":" behavior on the ordinal scale when unexpected categories must not\nsilently join the domain."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./themes-and-styling.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Themes and Styling"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./accessibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the surrounding policies."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"automatic-categorical-color","text":"Automatic categorical color","level":2},{"id":"quantitative-color","text":"Quantitative color","level":2},{"id":"automatic-color-legend","text":"Automatic color legend","level":2},{"id":"explicit-gradient-legend","text":"Explicit gradient legend","level":2},{"id":"controlled-interactive-legend","text":"Controlled interactive legend","level":2},{"id":"direct-labels-versus-legends","text":"Direct labels versus legends","level":2},{"id":"theme-and-accessibility-rules","text":"Theme and accessibility rules","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nColor has one semantic path. A mark's `color` channel contributes values to the\nchart-level `color` scale and legend. `z` partitions series or interaction\ngroups and supplies the color value only when `color` is omitted. `fill` and\n`stroke` are final paint overrides; using either bypasses scale mapping for\nthat paint.\n\n## Automatic categorical color\n\nWhen marks emit categorical color values and no color scale is supplied,\nTanStack Charts uses the chart theme palette. This is the convenient default\nfor a small, stable set of categories.\n\nFor persistent product semantics, supply an explicit configured ordinal scale:\n\n```ts\nimport { colorLegend, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst series = ['core', 'react', 'octane'] as const\nconst color = scaleOrdinal<string, string>()\n  .domain(series)\n  .range(['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'])\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'value',\n      z: 'series',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: x,\n    y: y,\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: color,\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Package' }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe application owns the domain order and paint assignment. This prevents a\ncategory from changing color when data is filtered or reordered.\n\n## Quantitative color\n\nSupply a D3 color-scale factory and put the numeric field on the mark's\n`color` channel:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleSequential } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { interpolateBlues } from 'd3-scale-chromatic'\nimport { colorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst color = {\n  scale: () => scaleSequential(interpolateBlues),\n  legend: colorLegend({\n    label: 'Requests per minute',\n    format: (value) => value.toLocaleString(),\n  }),\n}\n```\n\nThe factory keeps the interpolator and lets the chart infer the numeric domain\nfrom color-channel values. Continuous and quantize factories infer a finite\nextent. Quantile factories receive the complete observed numeric population,\nincluding duplicates. Threshold factories require an explicit domain because\ntheir cuts are policy, not an extent:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleThreshold } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst colors = ['#eff6ff', '#bfdbfe', '#60a5fa', '#1d4ed8']\n\nconst color = {\n  scale: scaleThreshold<number, string>,\n  domain: [5, 12, 24],\n  range: colors,\n  legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Incidents' }),\n}\n```\n\n`d3-scale` and `d3-scale-chromatic`, with their matching type packages, are\noptional direct application dependencies for these quantitative mappings.\nThey are never pulled into charts that do not import them. The\n[scale guide](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) owns the install and API-reference\nlinks.\n\n## Automatic color legend\n\n`colorLegend` reads the resolved scale:\n\n- categorical scales render labeled swatches;\n- continuous scales render a sampled ramp;\n- quantize, quantile, and threshold scales render exact bins and boundaries.\n\nOptions:\n\n- `label`: optional legend title;\n- `itemWidth`: minimum categorical item width;\n- `items`: categorical item presentation from `colorLegendItems()`;\n- `width`: preferred quantitative legend width;\n- `format`: numeric boundary formatter;\n- `placement`: `top` by default or `bottom`.\n\nThe legend reserves its own layout height. It is visual guidance and is hidden\nfrom the SVG accessibility tree; essential category meaning should also be\navailable through direct labels, surrounding HTML, or a table.\n\nThe default categorical legend stretches equal-width item columns. Pass\n`colorLegendItems()` to configure compact rows, typography, spacing, label\npaint, or indicator shape. `justify: 'start'` and `'center'` use the chart\nhost's text measurer, including configured typography, to wrap formatted\nlabels responsively:\n\n```ts\nimport { colorLegend, colorLegendItems } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\ncolorLegend({\n  placement: 'bottom',\n  items: colorLegendItems<'Revenue' | 'Orders'>({\n    justify: 'center',\n    gap: 20,\n    rowGap: 10,\n    indicator: {\n      width: 20,\n      height: 14,\n      gap: 6,\n      shape: (series) => (series === 'Revenue' ? 'line-dot' : 'square'),\n    },\n    label: {\n      fontSize: 14,\n      fill: (_series, { color }) => color,\n    },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n`dot`, `square`, `line`, and `line-dot` are renderer-neutral scene shapes.\nFor another symbol, provide `indicator.render` to `colorLegendItems()`; its\ncontext contains the resolved item color and the indicator bounds. The legend\nstill owns row measurement and wrapping.\n\n```ts group=automatic-color-legend env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { colorLegend, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'week',\n      y: 'downloads',\n      z: 'package',\n      strokeWidth: 2.5,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2),\n      axis: { label: 'Week' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { ticks: { count: 5 }, label: 'Downloads' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  color: { legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Package' }) },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=automatic-color-legend file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { week: 'May 4', package: 'core', downloads: 820 },\n  { week: 'May 11', package: 'core', downloads: 960 },\n  { week: 'May 18', package: 'core', downloads: 1_140 },\n  { week: 'May 25', package: 'core', downloads: 1_280 },\n  { week: 'May 4', package: 'react', downloads: 610 },\n  { week: 'May 11', package: 'react', downloads: 730 },\n  { week: 'May 18', package: 'react', downloads: 810 },\n  { week: 'May 25', package: 'react', downloads: 940 },\n]\n```\n\n[Open the grouped-bar catalog case](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/bar-grouped/).\n\n## Explicit gradient legend\n\n`colorGradientLegend` requires a numeric color-scale domain. Options:\n\n- `label`: optional title;\n- `steps`: rendered color samples, with a minimum of two;\n- `width`: preferred width capped by the chart;\n- `format`: formatter for the domain endpoints;\n- `placement`: `top` by default or `bottom`.\n\nUse `colorGradientLegend` only when a discrete scale should intentionally be\nshown as a sampled ramp. It requires a numeric domain and does not invent\nunits or semantic thresholds.\n\n## Controlled interactive legend\n\nUse `interactiveColorLegend` when a categorical color value is also the series\nidentity:\n\n```ts\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { interactiveColorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\nconst color = {\n  domain: ['core', 'react', 'octane'],\n  range: ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'],\n  legend: interactiveColorLegend({\n    visible: controlledSignal(visibleSeries, setVisibleSeries),\n    placement: 'bottom',\n    ariaLabel: 'Package visibility',\n  }),\n}\n```\n\nThe application stores `visibleSeries`; the legend proposes the next complete\narray in color-domain order. Filtering happens after scale resolution, so a\nhidden series keeps its color and does not change inferred position domains.\nThe browser host renders native pressed-state buttons and preserves their focus\nacross controlled updates. Static SVG rendering keeps a visual, noninteractive\nfallback.\n\nThis behavior applies to marks whose `color` channel defines series identity.\nKeep a separate `z` channel when grouping and color mean different things.\n\n## Direct labels versus legends\n\nPrefer direct labels when:\n\n- there are only a few lines or regions;\n- labels fit near endpoints;\n- the reader would otherwise move repeatedly between marks and a legend.\n\nPrefer a legend when:\n\n- the same category appears in many places;\n- marks are too dense for direct labels;\n- a shared mapping spans several views.\n\nIt is valid to use both when the legend establishes the complete domain and\ndirect labels help with the primary comparison.\n\n## Theme and accessibility rules\n\n- Keep semantic category colors stable across updates.\n- Test every supplied color against light and dark backgrounds.\n- Use a sequential scale for ordered magnitude and a diverging scale only when\n  a meaningful center exists.\n- Do not imply order with an unordered rainbow palette.\n- Do not use color as the only signal for selection, status, or error.\n- Use `unknown` behavior on the ordinal scale when unexpected categories must not\n  silently join the domain.\n\nSee [Themes and Styling](./themes-and-styling.md) and\n[Accessibility](./accessibility.md) for the surrounding policies.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Legends and Color\ndescription: Map categorical or quantitative values to color, render responsive legends, and preserve meaning across themes.\n---\n\nColor has one semantic path. A mark's `color` channel contributes values to the\nchart-level `color` scale and legend. `z` partitions series or interaction\ngroups and supplies the color value only when `color` is omitted. `fill` and\n`stroke` are final paint overrides; using either bypasses scale mapping for\nthat paint.\n\n## Automatic categorical color\n\nWhen marks emit categorical color values and no color scale is supplied,\nTanStack Charts uses the chart theme palette. This is the convenient default\nfor a small, stable set of categories.\n\nFor persistent product semantics, supply an explicit configured ordinal scale:\n\n```ts\nimport { colorLegend, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleOrdinal } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/ordinal'\n\nconst series = ['core', 'react', 'octane'] as const\nconst color = scaleOrdinal<string, string>()\n  .domain(series)\n  .range(['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'])\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'value',\n      z: 'series',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: x,\n    y: y,\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: color,\n    legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Package' }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe application owns the domain order and paint assignment. This prevents a\ncategory from changing color when data is filtered or reordered.\n\n## Quantitative color\n\nSupply a D3 color-scale factory and put the numeric field on the mark's\n`color` channel:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleSequential } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { interpolateBlues } from 'd3-scale-chromatic'\nimport { colorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst color = {\n  scale: () => scaleSequential(interpolateBlues),\n  legend: colorLegend({\n    label: 'Requests per minute',\n    format: (value) => value.toLocaleString(),\n  }),\n}\n```\n\nThe factory keeps the interpolator and lets the chart infer the numeric domain\nfrom color-channel values. Continuous and quantize factories infer a finite\nextent. Quantile factories receive the complete observed numeric population,\nincluding duplicates. Threshold factories require an explicit domain because\ntheir cuts are policy, not an extent:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleThreshold } from 'd3-scale'\n\nconst colors = ['#eff6ff', '#bfdbfe', '#60a5fa', '#1d4ed8']\n\nconst color = {\n  scale: scaleThreshold<number, string>,\n  domain: [5, 12, 24],\n  range: colors,\n  legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Incidents' }),\n}\n```\n\n`d3-scale` and `d3-scale-chromatic`, with their matching type packages, are\noptional direct application dependencies for these quantitative mappings.\nThey are never pulled into charts that do not import them. The\n[scale guide](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) owns the install and API-reference\nlinks.\n\n## Automatic color legend\n\n`colorLegend` reads the resolved scale:\n\n- categorical scales render labeled swatches;\n- continuous scales render a sampled ramp;\n- quantize, quantile, and threshold scales render exact bins and boundaries.\n\nOptions:\n\n- `label`: optional legend title;\n- `itemWidth`: minimum categorical item width;\n- `items`: categorical item presentation from `colorLegendItems()`;\n- `width`: preferred quantitative legend width;\n- `format`: numeric boundary formatter;\n- `placement`: `top` by default or `bottom`.\n\nThe legend reserves its own layout height. It is visual guidance and is hidden\nfrom the SVG accessibility tree; essential category meaning should also be\navailable through direct labels, surrounding HTML, or a table.\n\nThe default categorical legend stretches equal-width item columns. Pass\n`colorLegendItems()` to configure compact rows, typography, spacing, label\npaint, or indicator shape. `justify: 'start'` and `'center'` use the chart\nhost's text measurer, including configured typography, to wrap formatted\nlabels responsively:\n\n```ts\nimport { colorLegend, colorLegendItems } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\ncolorLegend({\n  placement: 'bottom',\n  items: colorLegendItems<'Revenue' | 'Orders'>({\n    justify: 'center',\n    gap: 20,\n    rowGap: 10,\n    indicator: {\n      width: 20,\n      height: 14,\n      gap: 6,\n      shape: (series) => (series === 'Revenue' ? 'line-dot' : 'square'),\n    },\n    label: {\n      fontSize: 14,\n      fill: (_series, { color }) => color,\n    },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n`dot`, `square`, `line`, and `line-dot` are renderer-neutral scene shapes.\nFor another symbol, provide `indicator.render` to `colorLegendItems()`; its\ncontext contains the resolved item color and the indicator bounds. The legend\nstill owns row measurement and wrapping.\n\n```ts group=automatic-color-legend env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { colorLegend, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'week',\n      y: 'downloads',\n      z: 'package',\n      strokeWidth: 2.5,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2),\n      axis: { label: 'Week' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { ticks: { count: 5 }, label: 'Downloads' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  color: { legend: colorLegend({ label: 'Package' }) },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=automatic-color-legend file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { week: 'May 4', package: 'core', downloads: 820 },\n  { week: 'May 11', package: 'core', downloads: 960 },\n  { week: 'May 18', package: 'core', downloads: 1_140 },\n  { week: 'May 25', package: 'core', downloads: 1_280 },\n  { week: 'May 4', package: 'react', downloads: 610 },\n  { week: 'May 11', package: 'react', downloads: 730 },\n  { week: 'May 18', package: 'react', downloads: 810 },\n  { week: 'May 25', package: 'react', downloads: 940 },\n]\n```\n\n[Open the grouped-bar catalog case](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/bar-grouped/).\n\n## Explicit gradient legend\n\n`colorGradientLegend` requires a numeric color-scale domain. Options:\n\n- `label`: optional title;\n- `steps`: rendered color samples, with a minimum of two;\n- `width`: preferred width capped by the chart;\n- `format`: formatter for the domain endpoints;\n- `placement`: `top` by default or `bottom`.\n\nUse `colorGradientLegend` only when a discrete scale should intentionally be\nshown as a sampled ramp. It requires a numeric domain and does not invent\nunits or semantic thresholds.\n\n## Controlled interactive legend\n\nUse `interactiveColorLegend` when a categorical color value is also the series\nidentity:\n\n```ts\nimport { controlledSignal } from '@tanstack/charts/interaction/signal'\nimport { interactiveColorLegend } from '@tanstack/charts/legend'\n\nconst color = {\n  domain: ['core', 'react', 'octane'],\n  range: ['#2563eb', '#f97316', '#10b981'],\n  legend: interactiveColorLegend({\n    visible: controlledSignal(visibleSeries, setVisibleSeries),\n    placement: 'bottom',\n    ariaLabel: 'Package visibility',\n  }),\n}\n```\n\nThe application stores `visibleSeries`; the legend proposes the next complete\narray in color-domain order. Filtering happens after scale resolution, so a\nhidden series keeps its color and does not change inferred position domains.\nThe browser host renders native pressed-state buttons and preserves their focus\nacross controlled updates. Static SVG rendering keeps a visual, noninteractive\nfallback.\n\nThis behavior applies to marks whose `color` channel defines series identity.\nKeep a separate `z` channel when grouping and color mean different things.\n\n## Direct labels versus legends\n\nPrefer direct labels when:\n\n- there are only a few lines or regions;\n- labels fit near endpoints;\n- the reader would otherwise move repeatedly between marks and a legend.\n\nPrefer a legend when:\n\n- the same category appears in many places;\n- marks are too dense for direct labels;\n- a shared mapping spans several views.\n\nIt is valid to use both when the legend establishes the complete domain and\ndirect labels help with the primary comparison.\n\n## Theme and accessibility rules\n\n- Keep semantic category colors stable across updates.\n- Test every supplied color against light and dark backgrounds.\n- Use a sequential scale for ordered magnitude and a diverging scale only when\n  a meaningful center exists.\n- Do not imply order with an unordered rainbow palette.\n- Do not use color as the only signal for selection, status, or error.\n- Use `unknown` behavior on the ordinal scale when unexpected categories must not\n  silently join the domain.\n\nSee [Themes and Styling](./themes-and-styling.md) and\n[Accessibility](./accessibility.md) for the surrounding policies.\n"}
